            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706540Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"text\": \"I would need to know why CBP recruitment is \\\"thin\\\" Is it a budgetary issue and why are the announcements with stated openings not being increased?\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.assistant.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706573Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"reasoningContent\": {\"reasoningText\": {\"text\": \"\", \"signature\": \"<stripped>"}}}, {\"text\": \"Not available\\nThe data cannot say why CBP Officer recruitment is thin or why announcements carry the openings they do. The records hold separation categories and posting openings, not reasons, and budget and appropriations are outside this review.\\n\\nThe figures already in this review show what \\\"thin\\\" means here. On August 1, 2026, eight CBP Officer announcements with stated openings were open, totaling eight openings, against 954 separations over the twelve months to August 2026, a coverage of 0.8%. Three more announcements say \\\"Many\\\" and are not counted, so the eight is a floor. Of the 954 separations, 586 were retirements.\\n\\nNearest answerable variants: the coverage ratio for CBP Officers at earlier dates, to show whether advertising has thinned over the year, or the separation categories by month, to show whether retirements are rising. I read \\\"thin\\\" as stated openings against separations; if you meant the number of announcements instead, say so.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"answer_value\\\": null, \\\"cited_tools\\\": []}\\n```\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.user.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706580Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"text\": \"Number of announcements would be helpful.\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.assistant.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706604Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"reasoningContent\": {\"reasoningText\": {\"text\": \"\", \"signature\": \"<stripped>"}}}, {\"text\": \"Verified\\nOn August 1, 2026, 11 CBP Officer announcements were open (the 1895 series, including supervisory and program-manager titles). Eight state a number of openings, totaling eight, and three say \\\"Many.\\\" Five give no state and two list Virginia. Because the count is an upper bound, announcements filled or cancelled before their closing dates are not visible. I read this as announcements open on August 1, 2026; if you meant the number opened over the trailing twelve months, say so.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"answer_value\\\": 11, \\\"cited_tools\\\": [\\\"workforce__semantic__open_postings\\\"]}\\n```\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.user.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706610Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"text\": \"Would want to know what many means\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.assistant.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706632Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"reasoningContent\": {\"reasoningText\": {\"text\": \"\", \"signature\": \"<stripped>"}}}, {\"text\": \"Not available\\n\\\"Many\\\" is the posting's own wording for an unstated number of openings, and the data holds only that word, so it cannot tell you whether a posting means two openings or two hundred. The three CBP Officer announcements that say \\\"Many\\\" carry control numbers 878058100 (grades 5 to 7), 878058600 (grade 9) and 875468500 (grade 13), and the openings figure is on each posting on USAJOBS. Until those are read, the eight stated openings are the only countable figure, and the 0.8% coverage is a floor.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"answer_value\\\": null, \\\"cited_tools\\\": []}\\n```\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.user.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.706640Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"text\": \"When OPM collects their data by grade is it possible to find out which positions are applicants not applying to? Trying to find out if the higher graded positions are the hardest to fill.  Would like to know if the lower graded positions are tough to get applicants.\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.choice",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:18.520603Z",
            "attributes": {
                "finish_reason": "end_turn",
                "message": "[{\"reasoningContent\": {\"reasoningText\": {\"text\": \"\", \"signature\": \"<stripped>"}}}, {\"text\": \"Not available\\nThe review's data has no applicant records, so it cannot show which positions applicants skip, and it has no grade field for staff to test whether higher grades are harder to fill. Posting counts do not measure difficulty either: an announcement can be open for weeks and still draw applicants.\\n\\nThe nearest answerable view is the grades on the 11 CBP Officer announcements open on August 1, 2026. Eight state openings and sit at grades 12 to 14 (three at 14, three at 13, two at 12). The three \\\"Many\\\" announcements sit at grades 5 to 7, 9 and 13, so the only lower-grade announcements open that day have no stated openings. I read \\\"hard to fill\\\" as openings advertised without applicant data; if you want the advertised grade mix over time, say so.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"answer_value\\\": null, \\\"cited_tools\\\": []}\\n```\"}]"
            }
        }
    ],
