        {
            "name": "gen_ai.assistant.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.703491Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"reasoningContent\": {\"reasoningText\": {\"text\": \"\", \"signature\": \"<stripped>"}}}, {\"text\": \"Not available\\nRetirement eligibility by occupation, projected over the next several years, and a Groups/Divisions/Branches breakdown are not in the data. The records carry years of federal service but no age or birth date, so eligibility cannot be computed, and the data has no organizational hierarchy below the component and occupation series. The staffing plan and postings align to component and occupation only, so the hiring pipeline can be matched at that level and no lower.\\n\\nAs a proxy, CBP as a whole had 43,890 of 70,843 staff (62.0%) with 10 or more years of service in the August 2026 snapshot, including 20,476 (28.9%) with 20 or more. Long service indicates retirement exposure but does not establish eligibility, and the data has no forward projection. The nearest answerable variants are the tenure profile by occupation series for CBP (for example CBP Officers, 1895, and Border Patrol agents, 1896), or the retirement-category separation counts by occupation for recent months.\\n\\nI read \\\"all areas in CPB\\\" as all CBP occupations as of the August 2026 snapshot; if you meant CBP Officers only, or a different component, say so.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"answer_value\\\": null, \\\"cited_tools\\\": []}\\n```\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.user.message",
            "timestamp": "2026-11-02T00:01:09.703504Z",
            "attributes": {
                "content": "[{\"text\": \"Just need CPB occupations but now that you asked, it might be useful to know how CBP compares to other components.\"}]"
            }
        },
        {
            "name": "gen_ai.assistant.message",
